# Interfaces Provide Powerful Abstraction Without Concrete Implementation

Interfaces in programming languages like TypeScript serve as pure contracts that define behavior without specifying implementation details. They're fundamental to writing flexible, maintainable code.

An interface establishes what methods and properties exist on objects, ensuring type consistency throughout your codebase. When you declare a type satisfies an interface (`interface SomeType implements SomeInterface {}`), TypeScript verifies that all required members are present and of the correct types.

Interfaces enable multiple inheritance patterns - an object can implement multiple interfaces simultaneously, combining different behavioral contracts. This prevents the diamond inheritance problems that plague class hierarchies.

The syntax is remarkably clean:
```typescript
interface Animal {
  sound(): string;
}

interface Feline<T extends string> {
  verticalJumpHeight?: number;
  favoriteFoods?: readonly (T | "fish")[];
}

class Cat implements Animal & Feline extends AnimalBase implements Interface<T, U> {
  constructor(public name: string = "spot") {}

  sound(): string { return `${this.name}: meow!`; }

  // Optional property - doesn't need explicit implementation
}
```

Interfaces also enable duck typing patterns - objects automatically satisfy interfaces they happen to match. Your `collections` object probably implements `Iterable` and `Destructible` even without explicit declarations.

Use `interface` for defining pure behavioral contracts that multiple unrelated types can share.

