# Understanding TypeScript Interfaces

TypeScript interfaces define contracts for objects - they specify what properties and methods must exist and what types they should have. Interfaces help ensure type consistency throughout your codebase.

The interface pattern allows for multiple inheritance patterns. An object can implement multiple interfaces simultaneously, combining different behavioral contracts. This is a powerful feature that prevents the diamond inheritance problems that plague class hierarchies.

Interfaces enable duck typing patterns - objects automatically satisfy interfaces they happen to match. Your `collections` object probably implements `Iterable` and `Destructible` even without explicit declarations.

The syntax is remarkably clean and expressive, allowing you to define complex type relationships that would be difficult to express with classes alone.

Here's a simple example:
```typescript
interface Animal {
  sound(): string;
  move(distanceInMeters: number): void;
}

interface Feline extends Animal {
  verticalJumpHeight?: number;
  favoriteFoods?: readonly ("fish" | "mice" | "birds")[];
}

class Cat implements Feline {
  constructor(public name: string = "spot") {}

  sound(): string { return `${this.name}: meow!`; }

  move(distanceInMeters: number): void {
    console.log(`${this.name} moves ${distanceInMeters}m`);
  }

  verticalJumpHeight = 3;
  favoriteFoods = ["fish", "mice"];
}
```

This demonstrates how interfaces can be extended and implemented to create flexible, maintainable code structures.

