TypeScript interfaces provide elegant contracts for objects that specify required properties and methods without implementation details. They're fundamental to writing consistent and maintainable TypeScript code.

Interfaces ensure type consistency throughout your codebase by enforcing that objects must have all specified properties. When you declare `collections satisfies Iterable<string>` and `collections` actually implements iterable methods (`collections[Symbol.iterator]`, `.map`, `.filter`), TypeScript verifies everything matches correctly.

```typescript
interface Database<T> {
  get(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  set(id: Pick<T, "id"> & Partial<Omit<T, "id">>): Promise<void>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}

const memoryStorage: Database<{id: string}> = Redis.createClient(6379);
```

The interface pattern enables clean multiple inheritance patterns. Your `collections` object likely implements multiple interfaces simultaneously - perhaps `Iterable<Collection>`, `Store<Collection>`, and `Serializable`. This combination approach prevents class hierarchy explosions while maintaining behavioral contracts.

Duck typing becomes explicit with interfaces - when TypeScript sees your array, generator, or whatever object has `length`, `.map`, and `[index]`, it infers appropriate interfaces. You don't need `collections satisfies Iterable` if the runtime prototype already matches.

```typescript
interface SettingsLoader {
  load(): Promise<Settings>;
  default?: Partial<Settings>;
  onError(e: Error): void;
}
```

Interfaces allow sophisticated type relationships that pure class hierarchies struggle to express.
